<h2>Business Interruption: The Part of the Claim That Takes Longest</h2>
<p>Business income coverage is designed to replace net income lost and continuing expenses while the property is being restored. In practice, it is often the most contested part of a commercial claim because it depends on projections, financial records and the length of the restoration period.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Coverage</th><th>What it generally addresses</th><th>Key questions</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Business income</td><td>Lost net income plus continuing normal expenses during the period of restoration</td><td>Waiting period, how long repairs should reasonably take, what sales would have been</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extra expense</td><td>Reasonable added costs to keep operating, such as temporary space or equipment rental</td><td>Whether the expense reduced the overall loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Civil authority</td><td>Income lost when government orders block access because of nearby damage</td><td>Distance limits, duration and the cause of the order</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contingent business income</td><td>Losses caused by damage at a key supplier’s or customer’s property</td><td>Only if endorsed; which dependent properties qualify</td></tr>
<tr><td>Utility service interruption</td><td>Losses from an off-site power, water or communications failure</td><td>Only if endorsed; whether physical damage to utility property is required</td></tr>
</tbody>
