<p>Port Arthur’s economy leans on refining, petrochemicals, shipping and the contractors and suppliers that support them. After a hurricane, a business can lose revenue because its own building was damaged, because a major customer shut down, or because roads and utilities were out. Those are three different coverage questions. Sorting them out early prevents a claim from being built on the wrong one.</p>

<h2>Physical Damage on Commercial Buildings</h2>
<p>Commercial roofs and structures in the Golden Triangle bring their own estimating issues.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Low-slope roofs</strong> such as TPO, modified bitumen and built-up systems can trap water under the membrane after wind damage, and wet insulation may not be visible from above.</li>
<li><strong>Metal buildings</strong> show wind damage at panel seams, fasteners, trim and doors; hail can dent panels in ways that raise cosmetic-damage exclusions.</li>
<li><strong>Rooftop HVAC units</strong>, signage and lighting are often damaged and often overlooked.</li>
<li><strong>Interior build-out</strong> may belong to the tenant, the landlord or both depending on the lease.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our pages on <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage claims</a> go deeper into the building side.</p>

