<h2>Multifamily Properties</h2>
<p>Apartment owners juggle building damage, tenant displacement and lost rents at the same time. Water that enters one unit can travel into the units below, and a single roof failure may affect dozens of tenants. Loss of rents coverage, where included, typically covers rental income lost while units are untenantable. Good records of occupancy and rent rolls before the loss make that portion of the claim much easier to support.</p>

<h2>HOAs and Condominium Associations</h2>
<p>Association claims raise a recurring question: who insures what? The governing documents and the master policy decide whether the association covers only the exterior shell, the structure up to bare walls, or original interior finishes as well. Unit owners then insure the rest. We work with boards and managers to document common-element damage across every building, track separate deductibles and keep the claim organized when many owners are asking questions at once.</p>

<h2>Policy Provisions That Can Change the Outcome</h2>
<h3>Coinsurance</h3>
<p>If the building limit is below the policy’s required percentage of value, the payment may be reduced. This is worth checking before a loss.</p>
<h3>Percentage deductibles</h3>
<p>Named-storm and wind/hail deductibles on commercial policies are often a percentage of the insured value and may apply per building. Use our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> to see how that math works.</p>
<h3>Separate wind and flood coverage</h3>
<p>As with homes, commercial property in Jefferson County may carry a separate TWIA or private wind policy, and flood is generally excluded from standard commercial forms. See our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page for how those policies interact.</p>

<h2>Documenting a Commercial Loss</h2>
<p>Commercial insurers expect more paperwork than residential carriers, and the income portion of the claim rises or falls on records. Before and during the claim, it helps to gather:</p>
<ul>
<li>The complete policy, including the declarations, statement of values and every endorsement</li>
<li>Building plans, prior roof reports and maintenance records, if available</li>
<li>Photos and video of each building, roof, rooftop unit and interior space</li>
<li>Profit and loss statements, tax returns and monthly sales for prior periods</li>
<li>Payroll records and a list of expenses that continued during the shutdown</li>
<li>Receipts for extra expenses, such as temporary space, generators or expedited shipping</li>
<li>Leases, rent rolls and occupancy records for multifamily and tenant-occupied property</li>
</ul>
<p>A Port Arthur Public Adjuster can help organize this information so the insurer receives a complete picture rather than a stream of partial submissions, which is a common reason commercial claims slow down. If your claim has stalled, see our page on <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>How We Approach Commercial Claims</h2>
<p>As a Port Arthur Public Adjuster, we start with the full policy, including every endorsement and the schedule of locations. We then inspect and document the buildings, prepare an Xactimate estimate, and work with your accountant on the business income figures. We present the claim to the insurer and keep communication in writing. We cannot guarantee a result, and coverage always depends on the policy and facts.</p>
<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. To talk through a commercial loss, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call [[phone]].</p>
